= Solution
{parent=i}

Write
$$
S(x):=\forall y(E(x,y)\to y=x),\qquad R(x):=\exists y(E(x,y)\land y\ne x).
$$
These are <first-order formulas> in the original language, not new predicate symbols. An axiomatization consists of the <equivalence relation> axioms for $E$, the bound
$$
\forall x\forall y\forall z\bigl((E(x,y)\land E(x,z))\to(y=z\lor y=x\lor z=x)\bigr),
$$
and, for every $n\geq1$, the two sentences
$$
\exists x_1\cdots x_n\left(\bigwedge_iS(x_i)\land\bigwedge_{i<j}x_i\ne x_j\right),
$$
$$
\exists x_1\cdots x_n\left(\bigwedge_iR(x_i)\land\bigwedge_{i<j}\neg E(x_i,x_j)\right).
$$
The first scheme supplies infinitely many singleton classes. The second supplies infinitely many distinct non-singleton classes; the size bound makes these doubletons.

Every countable <first-order model> has countably infinitely many classes of each size. A bijection between singleton classes, and another between doubleton classes followed by a bijection inside each matched pair, gives an <isomorphism> between any two countable <first-order models>. To infer completeness, suppose a sentence had different truth values in two arbitrary <first-order models>. The <downward Lowenheim-Skolem theorem> supplies countable <elementary substructures> of those <first-order models>, which would still disagree on the sentence, contradicting the <isomorphism> just constructed. Thus all <first-order models> satisfy the same sentences. Since the original <first-order structure> satisfies the axioms, **these axioms form a complete axiomatization of its <first-order theory>**. This is the <theory of infinitely many singleton and doubleton equivalence classes>.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**No.** Let $S$ be the displayed infinite axiom set. Any finite subset $S_0\subseteq S$ mentions only finitely many of the two infinitude requirements. Choose $N\geq1$ at least as large as every index mentioned. A finite <equivalence relation> with exactly $N$ singleton classes and $N$ doubleton classes satisfies $S_0$, but is not a <first-order model> of the <complete theory>.

If a finite axiomatization existed, let $\sigma$ be the conjunction of its axioms. Then $S\models\sigma$, so the <compactness theorem> gives a finite $S_0\subseteq S$ with $S_0\models\sigma$. Every <first-order model> of $S_0$ would then satisfy the full <first-order theory>, contradicting the finite <first-order model> above. This is the <finite subaxiomatization from an equivalent finite theory> argument: **finite axiomatizability would force one finite fragment of the explicit axioms to suffice**.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Take a countable <elementary substructure> $\mathcal B'$ of $\mathcal A'$ containing the named constant, using the <downward Lowenheim-Skolem theorem>. Its reduct $\mathcal B$ has infinitely many singleton and doubleton classes. In particular it contains the partner of each of its non-singleton elements, and its interpretation of $P$ is exactly the singleton-class predicate.

Choose new distinct elements $u,v\notin B$. Define
$$
C=B\cup\{u\},\qquad D=B\cup\{u,v\}.
$$
In $\mathcal C$, make $u$ a new singleton class. In $\mathcal D$, make $\{u,v\}$ a new doubleton class; keep every old class of $\mathcal B$ unchanged. All three reducts are <first-order models> of the <complete theory>, and $\mathcal B\subseteq\mathcal C\subseteq\mathcal D$.

Expand $\mathcal C,\mathcal D$ by interpreting $P$ as singleton-class membership and retaining the original named constant from $B$. The resulting countable <first-order structures> $\mathcal C',\mathcal D'$ are each isomorphic to $\mathcal B'$: all have infinitely many classes of each size, and the bijections of classes can be chosen to preserve the constant and its class. Hence they are <first-order models> of $\operatorname{Th}(\mathcal A')$. Both inclusions $\mathcal B'\subseteq\mathcal C'$ and $\mathcal B'\subseteq\mathcal D'$ are genuine <structure embeddings>, since no class meeting $B$ was changed. The given <quantifier elimination>, together with the preceding implication to <first-order model> completeness, yields
$$
\mathcal B'\preceq\mathcal C',\qquad\mathcal B'\preceq\mathcal D'.
$$
Taking reducts gives $\boxed{\mathcal B\preceq\mathcal C,\quad\mathcal B\preceq\mathcal D}$. But
$$
\mathcal C\models\neg\exists y(E(u,y)\land y\ne u),\qquad\mathcal D\models\exists y(E(u,y)\land y\ne u),
$$
so $\boxed{\mathcal C\npreceq\mathcal D}$. There is no conflict with <quantifier elimination> in the expanded language: $P(u)$ changes truth value, so $\mathcal C'$ is not a substructure of $\mathcal D'$.

= iv
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The inclusion $\mathcal C\subseteq\mathcal D$ is a <structure embedding> between <first-order models> of the same <complete theory>, but it is not an <elementary embedding>, as the existential partner <first-order formula> at $u$ shows. Therefore **the <first-order theory> is not model-complete**. The facts $\mathcal B\preceq\mathcal C$ and $\mathcal B\preceq\mathcal D$ do not make the intervening inclusion elementary.

